        <div class="about-content">
            <h2>Who We Are</h2>
            <p>Toad Town Services is a one-person technology services business serving Rhinelander and the Northwoods of Wisconsin. I'm Nick, the owner and the person who shows up at your door.</p>

            <h2>Background</h2>
            <p>My career has taken a lot of turns, and most of them involved figuring out how things work and fixing them when they don't.</p>

            <p>I spent two years as Director of Operations and Technology at WXPR Public Radio, where I managed broadcast equipment, migrated the station's network infrastructure, and held FCC Chief Operator responsibility. I'm a Society of Broadcast Engineers Certified Radio Operator and hold a broadcast engineering certificate from the Cleveland Institute of Electronics.</p>

            <p>Before and after that, I worked in IT and data analytics — building reporting systems for healthcare organizations, managing networks and compliance, deploying and supporting business technology. I've also done hands-on work across manufacturing, marine repair, broadband installation, and construction.</p>

            <p>I have a BA in Music and Management Studies from St. Olaf College, where I spent four years as a recording engineer for the college's concert series.</p>

            <h2>Why Toad Town Services</h2>

            <p>There's no shortage of tech support options, but most are either big-box impersonal or narrowly specialized. I started Toad Town Services because I kept running into people — neighbors, community members, small organizations — who needed someone who could actually sit down with them, understand their situation, and solve the problem in front of them rather than hand it off or upsell them.</p>

            <p>I currently volunteer with my church's audio and streaming setup and build music library software for WXPR. That's not filler — it means I'm actively working on the same kinds of problems my clients bring to me.</p>

            <h2>What to Expect</h2>

            <p>Clear communication about what I'm doing and what it will cost. No unnecessary upsells. If I don't know or can't help with something, I'll tell you that too.</p>
            <h2 id="why-toad">Why &ldquo;Toad&rdquo;?</h2>
            <p>The first time I met my wife Julie, we were at a birthday party at a lake cabin, and a few of us went out to see what amphibians were singing in a nearby ditch. Later that year at college, I came across a small American toad on my way to visit the same group of friends, so I picked it up and presented it to Julie. Apparently a toad made a stronger impression than a bunch of flowers would have.</p>
            <p>Years later, we bought a school bus to convert into an RV. When it came time to paint it, we chose green, and the bus became the <em>Toad</em>.</p>
            <img src="/images/why-toad-bus.jpg" alt="Nick standing in front of Toad, the green school bus, at our Rhinelander property" class="photo" loading="lazy">
            <p>Our property in Rhinelander had something the bus didn't: real toads. They're cute (to us) and great for pest control, so we gave them some good habitat in the garden. A whole town of toads.</p>
            <p>When it came time to name the business, it wasn't much of a debate. Toad Town Services is a continuation of the same thread: something personal, a little unexpected, and easy to remember.</p>
            <img src="/images/toad_front.jpg" alt="An American toad" class="photo" loading="lazy">


        </div>

        {{ contactBox("Ready to Discuss Your Project?", "Reach out anytime for a free consultation. We'll talk through what you need and figure out the best path forward together.") }}
    </div>
